{
    "map": {
        "description": "Analyzes the control point coverage and identifies the areas that need additional control points to improve the block adjust result.",
        "in_control_points.descr": "<xdoc><p>The input control point feature class.</p><p>It is normally created from the Compute Tie Points or the Compute Control Points tool.</p></xdoc>",
        "in_control_points.title": "Input Control Points",
        "in_mask_dataset.descr": "<xdoc><p>A polygon feature class used to exclude areas that you do not want in the analysis of the control points computation.</p><p>The <span class=\"esri_fieldname\">mask</span> field can control the inclusion or exclusion of areas. A value of 1 indicates that the areas defined by the polygons (inside) will be excluded from the computation. A value of 2 indicates the defined polygons (inside) will be included in the computation while areas outside of the polygons will be excluded.</p></xdoc>",
        "in_mask_dataset.title": "Input Mask",
        "in_mosaic_dataset.descr": "The input mosaic dataset against which to analyze the control points.",
        "in_mosaic_dataset.title": "Input Mosaic Dataset",
        "maximum_level.descr": "<xdoc><p>The maximum number of images that can be overlapped when analyzing the control points.</p><p>For example, if there are four images in your mosaic dataset, and a maximum overlap value of 3 was specified, then there are ten different combinations that will appear in the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Overlap Window</span>. If the four images were named i1, i2, i3, and i4, the ten combinations that would appear are [i1, i2, i3], [i1 i2 i4], [i1 i3 i4], [i2 i3 i4], [i1, i2], [i1, i3], [i1, i4], [i2, i3], [i2, i4], and [i3, i4].</p></xdoc>",
        "maximum_level.title": "Maximum Overlap Level",
        "minimum_area.descr": "<xdoc><p>Specify the minimum percent that the overlap area must be, in relation to the image. Areas that are lower than the specified percent threshold will be excluded from the analysis.</p><p>Ensure that you do not have areas that are too small; otherwise, you will have small slivers being analyzed.</p></xdoc>",
        "minimum_area.title": "Minimum Overlap Area",
        "out_coverage_table.descr": "A polygon feature class output that contains the control point coverage and the percentage of the area within the corresponding image.",
        "out_coverage_table.title": "Output Control Point Coverage Feature Class",
        "out_overlap_table.descr": "A polygon feature class output that contains all the overlap areas between images.",
        "out_overlap_table.title": "Output Overlap Feature Class",
        "summary": "<xdoc><p>Analyzes the control point coverage and identifies the areas that need additional control points to improve the block adjust result.</p><p>The tool will check each image and provide the following:<ul><li>The number of control points for each image</li><li>The percentage of image covered by the control points (point distribution)</li><li>The overlap areas</li><li>The number of control points within overlap areas</li></ul></p></xdoc>",
        "title": "Analyze Control Points"
    }
}
